        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-3{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=-7{x}^{3}$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{\u2032}={f}^{\u2032}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation",
        ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{4})}^{\u2032} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-12{x}^{3} \\\\  & \\ne -7{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{4}$ and ${y}^{\u2032}=-12{x}^{3}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ -12{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4(-3{x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ -12{x}^{3} & =-12{x}^{3}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-3{x}^{4}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
