  {
    "label": "259e28d6-9a87-555f-961a-c712d1726ac4",
    "record": {
      "knowledge_point": {
        "db_id": 17592186100888,
        "id": "201edc93-37b4-556a-aaea-4fcf9a01077a",
        "title": "Identifying a Solution to a First-Order Differential Equation"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "259e28d6-9a87-555f-961a-c712d1726ac4",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-5{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=-7x$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{\u2032}={f}^{\u2032}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation",
        ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-5{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & ={(-5{x}^{2})}^{\u2032} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-10x \\\\  & \\ne -7x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-5{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{\u2032}=-10x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -10x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2(-5{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ -10x & =-10x\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-5{x}^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
